 "vyzhimka": {
  "tekst": "I looked into Business Intelligence DNA (businessinteldna.com). It appears to be a real, very newly launched business operation, but I would distinguish “there are identifiable people and a legal entity behind it” from “it has an established track record.” The latter is not yet evident.\n\nThe people behind it appear to be Andrii Zhyla and Maryna (“Masha”) Zhyla. The site's own contract specifically says that the diagnostic and strategy work is personally performed by “Andrii Zhyla (strategy and positioning)” and “Maryna Zhyla (business economics, ex-CFO).” The contracting entity is identified as Wealthboosterpro LLC, doing business as Business Intelligence DNA, in Wyoming. ([businessinteldna.com](https://businessinteldna.com/terms))\n\nThere is independent evidence that these are real people with prior business histories. Andrii Zhyla has a substantial LinkedIn profile describing himself as an entrepreneur/finance professional with roughly 30 years of experience; his profile also links to Wealthboosterpro. ([linkedin.com](https://www.linkedin.com/in/azhyla?utm_source=openai)) Maryna Zhyla's LinkedIn identifies her with Wealthboosterpro Corp. and describes a long entrepreneurial background. ([linkedin.com](https://www.linkedin.com/in/marinazhyla?utm_source=openai)) Andrii also appears in corporate records unrelated to Business Intelligence DNA: for example, California records show him as CEO/CFO/director and registered agent of active film-production company Da Films Corp., incorporated in 2021. ([bizprofile.net](https://www.bizprofile.net/ca/santa-monica/da-films-corp?utm_source=openai)) UK Companies House records also show an Andrii Zhyla previously registered as a person with significant control of J.B. Finance LLP. ([find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/OC399405/filing-history?utm_source=openai))\n\nThere are, however, several reasons I would treat Business Intelligence DNA as a startup rather than an established consultancy:\n\n- The domain appears to be extremely new. A domain-registration index lists businessinteldna.com among domains newly registered on September 6, 2026. ([com.all-url.info](https://com.all-url.info/21/25/?utm_source=openai)) The site's first privacy-policy version is dated September 8, 2026, and its current terms took effect September 14, 2026. Today is September 16, 2026. ([businessinteldna.com](https://businessinteldna.com/terms)) In other words, this particular brand/site appears to be only about 10 days old.\n- I did not find a meaningful independent footprint yet for “Business Intelligence DNA”: no established review history, substantial press coverage, long-running customer-review trail, etc. That's understandable for something this new, but it means there isn't much outside evidence with which to validate customer experiences.\n- The Casper address should not be interpreted as their actual operating office. 5830 E 2nd St, Suite 7000 is used by Republic Registered Agent LLC and many unrelated Wyoming companies. Government documents show Republic Registered Agent at that exact address. ([sec.gov](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1945868/000194586823000006/exhibit_d_23.pdf?utm_source=openai)) BBB likewise describes that address, in connection with another company, as a commercial registered-agent location rather than typically a physical business location. ([bbb.org](https://www.bbb.org/us/wy/casper/profile/online-retailer/berdexa-commerce-llc-0805-46153155?utm_source=openai)) The Zhylas appear to be based in the Los Angeles area according to their profiles. ([linkedin.com](https://www.linkedin.com/in/azhyla?utm_source=openai))\n- I wasn't able through the indexed Wyoming sources to independently retrieve Wealthboosterpro LLC's current Wyoming Secretary of State record. Therefore I would not treat the site's statement that the LLC is registered and current as independently verified yet.\n\nOn the positive side, the site is unusually specific about the legal and commercial terms. It identifies its contracting entity, the two people providing the work, prices, refund/process guarantees, liability limits, contractors, Stripe payment processing, and governing law. For example, it currently lists a $500 diagnostic service, implementation starting at $5,000, and separate AI voice-agent offerings. ([businessinteldna.com](https://businessinteldna.com/terms)) It also provides a detailed privacy policy naming its infrastructure/providers—including Netlify, Anthropic, Stripe, Google, ElevenLabs, Twilio, Calendly and Zoom—and describes what data each receives. ([businessinteldna.com](https://businessinteldna.com/privacy)) Those disclosures are more substantive than I would normally expect from a throwaway scam site.\n\nMy assessment: I don't see evidence that this is an anonymous scam operation. There are identifiable principals with a verifiable business history, and the website provides unusually detailed contractual disclosures. But Business Intelligence DNA itself is brand-new, so claims about its effectiveness don't yet have an independently verifiable track record. “Legitimate startup run by identifiable people” is supportable from what I found; “established/proven consultancy” is not yet supportable.\n\nIf you're considering paying them $500 or, particularly, $5,000+, I would do one additional check before doing so: obtain the Wealthboosterpro LLC Wyoming filing ID/certificate of good standing and ask for 2–3 verifiable prior client references or examples of comparable paid implementations. I would also ensure that the name on the Stripe charge/invoice is Wealthboosterpro LLC and pay by credit card rather than wire/crypto.\n\nIf you want, I can dig one level deeper and investigate Andrii and Maryna Zhyla's business history—including Wealthboosterpro, previous companies, lawsuits/complaints, corporate registrations, and whether their claimed professional backgrounds check out.",
